<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">NATO</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as North Atlantic Treaty Organization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The North Atlantic Treaty Organization is a military alliance founded by the North Atlantic Treaty, signed in Washington on 4 April 1949 by twelve countries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Its members pledge that an armed attack on one is an attack on all. Created to deter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, it expanded into central and eastern Europe after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nato.jpg" alt="NATO" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NATO_OTAN_landscape_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>NATO has its roots in the Atlantic Charter, a 1941 agreement between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom. On 4 March 1947 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the United Kingdom signed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a>, an alliance against a possible attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and in March 1948 the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> extended it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benelux">Benelux</a> countries as the Brussels Treaty Organization, commonly called the Western Union.</p><p>Talks on a wider alliance including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> began that month, shaped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truman-doctrine">Truman Doctrine</a> of 1947 and the February 1948 coup d'état in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and led to the North Atlantic Treaty, signed at Washington on 4 April 1949 by twelve founding members: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Iceland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Luxembourg, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, Portugal, the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The treaty was largely dormant until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> led to an integrated military structure. Supreme Headquarters Allied Powers Europe was formed in 1951, the post of Secretary General was created in 1952, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> joined that year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> joined in May 1955, a major factor in the creation of the Soviet-dominated Warsaw Pact. Doubts about the credibility of NATO's defence led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to leave the military structure in 1966; it returned to the integrated command in 2009.</p><p>NATO conducted no military operations during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-war">Bosnian War</a> it began enforcing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> no-fly zone on 12 April 1993. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srebrenica">Srebrenica</a> genocide, a two-week bombing campaign, Operation Deliberate Force, began in August 1995 against the Army of the Republika Srpska, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayton-agreement">Dayton Agreement</a> followed in November 1995. NATO then deployed a peacekeeping force, IFOR, of almost 60,000 troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The treaty's central provision is that an armed attack on one member is an attack on all. Under Article 6 the area within which an attack permits that response includes member territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and the islands of the North Atlantic north of the Tropic of Cancer. The main headquarters are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. Twelve of the present 32 members joined in 1949; the other twenty joined in ten rounds of enlargement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “NATO” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Armenia ratified the Treaty on Conventional Armed Forces in Europe in 1992, acceded to the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty in 1993, joined the Collective Security Treaty Organisation and sent troops to <b>NATO</b>'s KFOR in Kosovo in 2004; Russia keeps a base at Gyumri as a deterrent against Turkey.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>By 2021 the armed forces had 126,000 active personnel and 17,000 paramilitary troops, a defence budget of $2.24 billion in 2020, 5.4 percent of GDP, and an arms industry of its own; Azerbaijan cooperates with <b>NATO</b> through the Partnership for Peace and sent peacekeepers to Iraq and Afghanistan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A founding member of <b>NATO</b> in 1949, Canada fought in the Korean War, joined the United States in the North American Air Defence Command in 1958, took part in many United Nations peacekeeping missions, and fought in Afghanistan from 2001.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>West Germany joined <b>NATO</b> in 1955, faced the Berlin Wall from 1961 and eased tensions with the East through Willy Brandt's Ostpolitik in the late 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Georgia sent up to 2,000 soldiers to Iraq and 1,560 to Afghanistan in 2013, the largest non-<b>NATO</b> contingent, where 32 died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1945 Luxembourg introduced conscription for a time, sent volunteers to fight in Korea alongside the Belgian battalion and has since kept a small professional army within <b>NATO</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Military</a></li></ul></section>
